! C22-6578 PROBLEM 4.8, "PRODUCT OF SQUARE MATRICES", printed p.42 (PDF
! 43), Method 1, read in the ink at 300 dpi.
!
!   "NxN full-word floating-point matrices L,R are stored row-wise
!   beginning at LMTRIX and RMTRIX respectively.  Create P=L*R and store
!   it row-wise beginning at PMTRIX."
!   "Method 1.  Use $2 for left matrix elements, $3 for right matrix
!   elements and $4 for product matrix elements.  Program generates
!   successive rows of the product matrix."
!   Comments: "(a) STRAP I does not perform multiplication of addresses,
!   but STRAP II will do it properly.  (b) XW2, XW3, and XW4 are not
!   destroyed and the program can be used repeatedly without
!   re-assembly or reloading into the machine."
!
! THE EIGHTEEN PRINTED STATEMENTS FROM "TI,3,XW2,$2" TO "ZERO DD(N),0"
! ARE AS PRINTED, "V-ICR,$3,N*N-1.0" and "DD(N),0" included: this deck is
! assembled by the host STRAP II (ASM=strap2), which multiplies addresses
! as comment (a) says STRAP II will, and builds a zero DD(N).  strap_asm
! refuses both (decks/repro_address_multiply.strap, repro_dd_n_zero.strap)
! and the earlier Chapter 4 decks substituted for them; this one need
! not.  The deck adds: "START CNOP" to name the unlabelled first
! statement for END; N, which the page uses and never defines (N SYN,8,
! an integer, as 4.6 defines it: "N SYN,8"); and the data.
!
! >>> AS PRINTED THE PROGRAM DOES NOT FORM L * R: "V+I,$3,N" AND
! "V+I,$2,N" ADVANCE N HALF WORDS, NOT N WORDS. <<<
!   An integer in a 19-bit address field counts half words: C28-6129
!   Section I printed pp.8-9, "INTEGER ADDRESSES" ("A 19-bit field
!   specifies half words so an integer here counts half words ... V+I,
!   $3, 13 ... is equivalent to 13 half words or location 6, bit position
!   32"); C22-6531 has the same table; V+I adds to value bits 0-18
!   (Reference Manual printed p.25).  The page knows the rule: 4.6
!   Method 2 comments "LVI,$2,8-1" with "'7 half-words", and this
!   program writes "V+I,$4,1.0" for one word.  So with N = 8 each
!   "advance $3 to next row" is 4 words, and "V-ICR,$3,N*N-1.0" is 64 half
!   words less 1.0, 31 words -- which brings $3 back to the next column
!   exactly, since 8 steps of 4 words are 32: the program is consistent
!   with itself and computes, for row i and column j,
!       P'[i][j] = sum over k of Lw[4i + k] * Rw[j + 4k]
!   with Lw, Rw the 64 words of L and R in storage order.  Every read
!   stays inside the two matrices (the largest index is 35).
!   "N SYN,8.0" (a bit address) does not repair it: the two V+I then
!   advance 8 words and the count fields still take 8, but STRAP II
!   multiplies "both bit address terms and integer terms ... as 24-bit
!   integers and the point is forgotten", the product "treated as an
!   integer" (C28-6129 Section II printed pp.35-36, whose example is
!   2.0 * 2.0 = 16384 bits), so N*N is 512 x 512, and strap2 assembles
!   V-ICR as 8190 half words.  The page's own way to a word count would
!   have been "(N*N)." (p.36: "enclosed in parentheses and followed by a
!   period ... treated like a standard binary bit address").
!   ONE READING LEFT OPEN: p.35 makes an exception for "immediate
!   operation address fields" mixing integers and bit addresses ("all
!   terms are considered to be bit addresses"); its example is a VFL
!   LI(BU,24,8), and strap2 does not apply it to V-ICR.  If it did apply,
!   N*N-1.0 would be .64 - 1.0 = 0 and the printed program would still not
!   form L * R.
!   THE DECK RUNS AS PRINTED, and corrected ("--corrected", three cards
!   replaced in a copy, tests/verify_problem_4_matrix.py CORRECTIONS):
!       V+I,$3,N          ->  V+I,$3,N+N          (N words)
!       V-ICR,$3,N*N-1.0  ->  V-ICR,$3,2*N*N-1.0  (N*N - 1 words)
!       V+I,$2,N          ->  V+I,$2,N+N
!
! WHAT THE PROGRAM DOES, by the Reference Manual (printed page = PDF - 3).
!   TI,3,XW2,$2       XW2, XW3, XW4 into $2, $3, $4.
!   SIX  SX,$2,XW22   $2's refill word is the row of L in hand.
!   LU   DL(U),ZERO   the double accumulator cleared (p.96).
!   LIFT LFT(U),0($2) L[i][k] to the factor register (p.98).
!        *+(N),0($3)  plus FT x R[k][j], 96 bits, unnormalized in the
!                     addition (p.98).
!        V+I,$3,N     $3 down a row of R (but see above).
!        CBR+,$2,LIFT $2 one word on ("Add one to value", bit 17,
!                     p.26), counted; branch while the count is not
!                     zero; refilled to the row's start at zero.
!        SRD(N),0($4) P[i][j], rounded and normalized (p.96); V+I,$4,1.0.
!        V-ICR,$3,... $3 back to the top of the next column, counted;
!                     BZXCZ while $3's count (8 a row) is not zero, and
!                     $3 refilled to RMTRIX at zero (XW3's refill is $).
!        V+I,$2,N     next row of L; CB,$4,SIX counts the rows.
!
! THE DATA: L and R are 8 x 8 integers in -3..3, none zero, drawn once
! from Python's random.Random(4824) (L's 64, then R's), chosen so that no
! element of either product is zero.  Every product and sum is an exact
! small integer, so the rounding in SRD(N) and the order of the
! additions cannot change a word, and each expected element is the
! normalized word of an integer (p.78).  THE EXPECTED VALUES are worked by
! tests/verify_problem_4_matrix.py from the words as assembled, with
! problem_3_expected.py's arithmetic, not by the model, and checked
! there against the integer sums; the printed program's by a trace of its
! statements, asserted to give L * R when corrected.
!
!   L
!        1   -1    1   -2    3    2   -2   -2
!        1    3   -2    3    2   -2    1    2
!       -2    3    2   -3   -3   -2   -3    1
!        1    3   -2   -3    3    2   -3   -1
!       -3   -1    1   -3    2    2    2   -2
!        2   -3    3   -3   -2   -2    2    2
!        3    2   -3    1    2   -2    2   -1
!       -3   -1   -1   -1    1   -2   -2   -3
!   R
!        3   -2    1   -1    1   -3    3   -2
!       -3    2    3    2   -2   -3   -1   -3
!        2    1    1    3    2    2   -1   -2
!        2   -3    1    1    3    2   -1    3
!       -2    1    2   -1   -2   -1   -3    3
!        2   -3    3    3    3    2   -1    1
!        2   -1    3   -3   -1    2   -1    1
!        3    2    1    1   -2    1    1   -2
!   P = L * R, the statement (the corrected program)
!       -8   -2    1    5    5   -7   -6    6
!       -4    4   14   -7  -15  -12   -4    3
!      -18   29  -14   18  -12  -11    7  -34
!      -27    9    7    7  -13  -28   -4   -6
!      -12    4    6   -3   -4   12  -18   12
!       25    8   -9  -10   -3    7   17  -20
!       -8   -4   10  -22  -14  -22    2    5
!      -29    9  -21   -7   -6   -4   -8   13
!   P', what the printed program stores (63 of 64 elements differ)
!        3   18    4    6    6  -16   -6  -21
!       34    9   -2   -1   -7   -9   25  -10
!       14  -25    4   -3    1   26   16   14
!       -6  -10   -3  -21   24  -23   -2   11
!      -16   12   11    4  -42   -2    4  -11
!       -9   10  -20  -16   19    1  -13   12
!       19    8    6   -3   -7   -1   11  -11
!        7  -29    3  -25    2   -1   12   15
!
!   Also checked: $2, $3, $4 and XW22 at the end, from the same trace, the
!   guards, and the halt PC.
!
! PASSED AGAINST THE MODEL AS IT STOOD, BOTH RUNS (64 elements, three
! index words, XW22, the guards and the PC): as printed the model stores
! exactly P' (63 of the 64 differ from L * R), and corrected, L * R.
!
! INJECTION, DATA SIDE, OBSERVED 2026-11-16:
!   L[0][0] 1 -> 2 ("LMTRIX  DD(N),1;" -> "DD(N),2;", by --sub on the copy
!   that runs; the expected words are worked from the deck as it
!   stands).  PREDICTED: P[0][j] += R[0][j] corrected, and P'[0][j] +=
!   Rw[j] as printed: row 0, eight elements, either way.
!   OBSERVED, both runs: FAIL P00-P07 and nothing else (as printed P00
!   6 for 3, (16)006C000000000000 for (16)004C000000000000; corrected P00
!   -5 for -8).

! --- printed p.42, Method 1 -------------------------------------------
START   CNOP
        TI,3,XW2,$2                     !'load three index registers
SIX     SX,$2,XW22
LU      DL(U),ZERO
LIFT    LFT(U),0($2)                    !'main loop
        *+(N),0($3)
VPI     V+I,$3,N                        !'advance $3 to next row
        CBR+,$2,LIFT                    !'advance $2 to next element
        SRD(N),0($4)                    !'new product matrix element
        V+I,$4,1.0
        V-ICR,$3,N*N-1.0
        BZXCZ,LU                        !'towards new product element of same row
        V+I,$2,N                        !'procedure for new row
        CB,$4,SIX;BEW,$
XW2     XW,LMTRIX,N,XW22
XW3     XW,RMTRIX,N,$
XW4     XW,PMTRIX,N,$
XW22    XW,0
ZERO    DD(N),0
! --- the deck's: N, which the page does not define, and the data ------
N       SYN,8
        SLC, 199.0
GUARD1  DD(BU,64),(16)A5A5A5A5A5A5A5A5
LMTRIX  DD(N),1;DD(N),-1;DD(N),1;DD(N),-2;DD(N),3;DD(N),2;DD(N),-2;DD(N),-2
        DD(N),1;DD(N),3;DD(N),-2;DD(N),3;DD(N),2;DD(N),-2;DD(N),1;DD(N),2
        DD(N),-2;DD(N),3;DD(N),2;DD(N),-3;DD(N),-3;DD(N),-2;DD(N),-3;DD(N),1
        DD(N),1;DD(N),3;DD(N),-2;DD(N),-3;DD(N),3;DD(N),2;DD(N),-3;DD(N),-1
        DD(N),-3;DD(N),-1;DD(N),1;DD(N),-3;DD(N),2;DD(N),2;DD(N),2;DD(N),-2
        DD(N),2;DD(N),-3;DD(N),3;DD(N),-3;DD(N),-2;DD(N),-2;DD(N),2;DD(N),2
        DD(N),3;DD(N),2;DD(N),-3;DD(N),1;DD(N),2;DD(N),-2;DD(N),2;DD(N),-1
        DD(N),-3;DD(N),-1;DD(N),-1;DD(N),-1;DD(N),1;DD(N),-2;DD(N),-2;DD(N),-3
RMTRIX  DD(N),3;DD(N),-2;DD(N),1;DD(N),-1;DD(N),1;DD(N),-3;DD(N),3;DD(N),-2
        DD(N),-3;DD(N),2;DD(N),3;DD(N),2;DD(N),-2;DD(N),-3;DD(N),-1;DD(N),-3
        DD(N),2;DD(N),1;DD(N),1;DD(N),3;DD(N),2;DD(N),2;DD(N),-1;DD(N),-2
        DD(N),2;DD(N),-3;DD(N),1;DD(N),1;DD(N),3;DD(N),2;DD(N),-1;DD(N),3
        DD(N),-2;DD(N),1;DD(N),2;DD(N),-1;DD(N),-2;DD(N),-1;DD(N),-3;DD(N),3
        DD(N),2;DD(N),-3;DD(N),3;DD(N),3;DD(N),3;DD(N),2;DD(N),-1;DD(N),1
        DD(N),2;DD(N),-1;DD(N),3;DD(N),-3;DD(N),-1;DD(N),2;DD(N),-1;DD(N),1
        DD(N),3;DD(N),2;DD(N),1;DD(N),1;DD(N),-2;DD(N),1;DD(N),1;DD(N),-2
PMTRIX  DD(BU,64,8),7;DD(BU,64,8),7;DD(BU,64,8),7;DD(BU,64,8),7;DD(BU,64,8),7;DD(BU,64,8),7;DD(BU,64,8),7;DD(BU,64,8),7
        DD(BU,64,8),7;DD(BU,64,8),7;DD(BU,64,8),7;DD(BU,64,8),7;DD(BU,64,8),7;DD(BU,64,8),7;DD(BU,64,8),7;DD(BU,64,8),7
        DD(BU,64,8),7;DD(BU,64,8),7;DD(BU,64,8),7;DD(BU,64,8),7;DD(BU,64,8),7;DD(BU,64,8),7;DD(BU,64,8),7;DD(BU,64,8),7
        DD(BU,64,8),7;DD(BU,64,8),7;DD(BU,64,8),7;DD(BU,64,8),7;DD(BU,64,8),7;DD(BU,64,8),7;DD(BU,64,8),7;DD(BU,64,8),7
        DD(BU,64,8),7;DD(BU,64,8),7;DD(BU,64,8),7;DD(BU,64,8),7;DD(BU,64,8),7;DD(BU,64,8),7;DD(BU,64,8),7;DD(BU,64,8),7
        DD(BU,64,8),7;DD(BU,64,8),7;DD(BU,64,8),7;DD(BU,64,8),7;DD(BU,64,8),7;DD(BU,64,8),7;DD(BU,64,8),7;DD(BU,64,8),7
        DD(BU,64,8),7;DD(BU,64,8),7;DD(BU,64,8),7;DD(BU,64,8),7;DD(BU,64,8),7;DD(BU,64,8),7;DD(BU,64,8),7;DD(BU,64,8),7
        DD(BU,64,8),7;DD(BU,64,8),7;DD(BU,64,8),7;DD(BU,64,8),7;DD(BU,64,8),7;DD(BU,64,8),7;DD(BU,64,8),7;DD(BU,64,8),7
GUARD2  DD(BU,64),(16)5A5A5A5A5A5A5A5A
        END, START
